  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A drying and cooling to seasonal temperatures\nafter this evening.\n\nThe boundary of a cold front stretches across Tennessee and \ninto the Carolinas this evening. An inverted trough over the \nmountains triggered a few storms earlier, but coverage will \ncontinue to shrink with the loss of evening heating. As a weak \nsoutheast wind develops along the eastern slopes of the \nnorthwest North Carolina Blue Ridge, a few isolated showers may \nform overnight, though confidence in persistent rainfall remains\nlow. By Monday and Tuesday, drier air will push into the \nregion, bringing more sunshine and noticeably lower humidity.\n\n\n\nKey Message 2: Heat builds again mid to late week but not as \nhot as last week. A chance of storms Thu-Sat.\n\n594dm heights return by midweek with more of a westerly flow. \nShould see temps warm about 5 to 10 degrees above normal, still \n\"hot\" for September but not looking as hot as last week.\n\nA front tracks southeast into the area Thursday with a decent \nshot of shower/storms. Depending on timing of this feature would\nnot be surprised to some stronger storms Thu afternoon/evening.\n\nFront track south into the Carolinas but stalls as it becomes \nparallel to upper flow. Another wave of low pressure across the \nsoutheast could keep shower chances around into the weekend.",
